<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Anhalt-Köthen</h1><p class="st-meta">1603 CE – 1847 CE · capital Köthen</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">One of the Anhalt principalities of central Germany, home of the Fruitbearing Society and of the court where Johann Sebastian Bach wrote much of his instrumental music.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-anhalt">Anhalt</a> was partitioned in 1603 among the sons of Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joachim-ernest-prince-of-anhalt">Joachim Ernst</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kothen">Köthen</a> fell to Ludwig, who founded the line of Anhalt-Köthen. Ludwig was a learned prince who in 1617 co-founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fruitbearing-society">Fruitbearing Society</a> (Fruchtbringende Gesellschaft), the first society devoted to refining the German language.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The principality was a small Calvinist state in the Saale-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> region, living from agriculture. Its court is best known for Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-prince-of-anhalt-kothen">Leopold</a>, a music lover who employed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-sebastian-bach">Johann Sebastian Bach</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapellmeister">Kapellmeister</a> from 1717 to 1723, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-concertos">Brandenburg Concertos</a> and much chamber music were composed. In 1665 the line of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-anhalt-plotzkau">Anhalt-Plötzkau</a> inherited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kothen">Köthen</a>, continuing it under a new branch.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kothen">Köthen</a> became a duchy in 1807 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon's</a> patronage and joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/german-confederation">German Confederation</a>. When its last duke died without heirs in 1847, the territory passed to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-anhalt-dessau">Anhalt-Dessau</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-anhalt-bernburg">Anhalt-Bernburg</a>, and was absorbed into the united <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-anhalt">Duchy of Anhalt</a> by 1863.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-anhalt-kothen.jpg" alt="Principality of Anhalt-Köthen" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: Germenfer · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Anhalt_Duchies.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1603 CE – 1847 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Köthen</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ludwig I, Leopold, Ferdinand Frederick</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Calvinism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Duchy of Anhalt-Köthen, Anhalt-Köthen</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1725&amp;focus=principality-of-anhalt-kothen" aria-label="Principality of Anhalt-Köthen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4950 388 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Anhalt-Köthen lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5270 644L5276 641M5270 644L5229 694M5270 644L5276 641M5270 644L5264 644"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5276 641h0M5229 694h0M5276 641h0M5264 644h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5270 644h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5270 644h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1725&amp;focus=principality-of-anhalt-kothen">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
